--- body ---
## Source
Found live during the 0216 dogfood migration: committing the refreshed
tickets/erg on a branch in ~/chemin-de-voix (default branch: master)
was blocked by the hook, and committing on master itself would have
been blocked too. Had to use --no-verify.

## Context
The pre-commit hook snippet compares against a hardcoded "main":

- src/go/install.go:84   `if [ "$branch" != "main" ]; then`
- src/go/assets/integration.md:36  (same snippet, documented form)

On repos whose default branch is master (or trunk, develop...), the
binary-commit gate can never be satisfied: every branch, including the
default one, is "not main", so the legitimate binary refresh on the
default branch is rejected and the documented policy ("commit the
binary on main") is unfollowable without --no-verify.

## Fix
Detect the default branch instead of hardcoding main. Options, in
preference order:
1. `git symbolic-ref --short refs/remotes/origin/HEAD` (strip origin/),
   falling back to "main" when there is no remote.
2. An .ergrc key (e.g. [hooks] default-branch) for offline repos.
Update both install.go (the installed hook) and the integration.md
snippet so the two stay coherent; CI self-coherence guard should catch
drift.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] Hook installed by `erg install --hooks` allows tickets/erg commit
      on the repo's actual default branch (master-default repo covered
      by a test)
- [ ] integration.md snippet matches the installed hook
- [ ] make check passes

## Plan

### Actions (file:line precision)

1. **src/go/install.go lines 80-108** — replace the `hookBody` const.
   The two lines to change are:
   - Line 80 (first comment): update to reflect dynamic detection.
   - Lines 83-88 (the if-block): replace
     ```
     branch=$(git branch --show-current)
     if [ "$branch" != "main" ]; then
     ```
     with:
     ```
     default_branch=$(git symbolic-ref --short refs/remotes/origin/HEAD 2>/dev/null | sed 's|^origin/||')
     default_branch=${default_branch:-main}
     branch=$(git branch --show-current)
     if [ "$branch" != "$default_branch" ]; then
     ```
   The fallback `${default_branch:-main}` handles repos with no remote (offline,
   bare, fresh `git init` before a first push).

2. **src/go/assets/integration.md lines 32-41** — update the `# >>> erg managed >>>` snippet
   block (lines 31-61 in the rendered file) to match the new hookBody exactly.
   The if-block starting at line 35 needs the same four-line replacement as Action 1.
   Line 32 comment also needs updating to mention dynamic detection.

3. **tests/test_install.sh** — add two new test cases AFTER the existing
   `erg-github` test (currently the last test, ending around line 381):
   - **master-default repo test**: `git init -b master`, install hook,
     stage `tickets/erg` on `master` branch → commit must SUCCEED (exit 0).
     Also verify: staging on a feature branch in that repo → commit must FAIL.
   - This test exercises the `git symbolic-ref` path when there IS a remote
     whose HEAD points to master.  Companion: test with NO remote (fallback to
     "main") using `git init -b main` (already covered by existing tests).

4. **tests/test_selfcoherence.sh** — add a new guard (after the existing
   tests, before the final summary) that extracts the shell block from
   `src/go/assets/integration.md` between the `# >>> erg managed >>>` and
   `# <<< erg managed <<<` markers and byte-compares it against the `hookBody`
   Go const as installed into a fresh hook.  Concretely:
   - Run `erg install --hooks` on a temp repo; extract the managed block from
     the resulting `.git/hooks/pre-commit`.
   - Extract the snippet block from `src/go/assets/integration.md` (strip the
     ` ``` ` fences and the marker lines themselves).
   - `cmp -s` the two extracts; fail if they differ.
   - Add a negative control: mutate one extract and verify cmp detects the
     difference.

### First test description

"master-default repo: `tickets/erg` commit allowed on master" in
`tests/test_install.sh`: create a git repo with `-b master`, add a remote
`origin` whose HEAD resolves to `master` (or skip the remote and rely on the
fallback path by naming the initial branch `main` in a no-remote scenario).
The simplest approach that exercises the real fix: `git init -b master` then
`git remote add origin .` + `git fetch origin` is heavy; instead use
`git symbolic-ref refs/remotes/origin/HEAD` set manually:
```sh
REPO=$(new_repo masterdefault)
git -C "$REPO" symbolic-ref refs/remotes/origin/HEAD refs/remotes/origin/master
$ERG install "$REPO" --hooks >/dev/null 2>&1
( cd "$REPO" && git config user.email t@t && git config user.name t )
( cd "$REPO" && git switch -q -c master 2>/dev/null || git checkout -q -b master 2>/dev/null || true )
# Stage tickets/erg on master → must succeed
( cd "$REPO" && git add tickets/erg && git commit -q -m "erg refresh on master" ) && rc=0 || rc=$?
# Expect exit 0
```

### Dependencies

None. No new Go packages, no new test files needed (both additions go into
existing test files already in TEST_SUITES).

### Tautology-scan verdict

**PASS — tests are non-tautological.**

- The master-repo test would FAIL with the current (broken) implementation
  because `branch=master != "main"` → hook exits 1. It would also FAIL with a
  trivially wrong implementation that always exits 1 (hook blocks everything).
  It would PASS only if the installed hook correctly detects `master` as the
  default branch.

- The coherence test in test_selfcoherence.sh compares the actual bytes of the
  installed hook's managed block against the actual bytes of the integration.md
  snippet. A wrong implementation where hookBody and integration.md diverge
  (e.g. the fix is applied to one but not the other) will be caught. A trivially
  passing implementation (always returning empty or the same string) would fail
  the negative control. The test does NOT merely check "both contain some
  string" — it does a full byte comparison after stripping fences/markers.
